=== Text #11 ===
> [September]’s Free Book of the Month is here. Get yours now.
The story of Joseph offers a vivid picture of redemption, reconciliation, and forgiveness.[ But] it’[s so] much more than that.
The Joseph story is sometimes described by scholars as ‘the Joseph cycle’. This is a helpful term. In Genesis, there are similarly ‘cycles’ of stories about Abraham (Gen. 12–25) and Jacob (Gen. 26–36); and in other parts of the Bible, there is a ‘David Cycle’ for example (1 Sam. 16–[1] Kgs. 2) and one about Elijah (1 Kgs. 17–[2] Kgs. 2). A ‘cycle’ in this sense is a series of connected and continuous narratives about a central figure, in which the component parts nevertheless have their own separate[ co]herence and integrity—like episodes in a TV drama series perhaps or like the individual parts in a ‘cycle’ of Medieval mystery plays.
